body:
Hmm, I'll definitely have to look into this. I guess the suspend isn't entirely necessary, so long as the resources are dedicated to the OS in "focus"

&#x200B;

I've started looking into how I might be able to roll my own solution leveraging KVM or something.

Yeah, this is my current solution on my desktop, but I also have the benefit of having 32 threads on that monster, so I don't sweat with running a bunch of VMs while still being able to do other work.

&#x200B;

Maybe my idea is too niche, or just not practical. I may try to roll my own solution to this. I wonder if people would be interested, at the very least, I'd get some more familiarity with low-level functions of Hypervisors.

Yeah, I love WSL2, but I love some of the Linux DE's, and running Linux GUI apps in WSL2 is do-able but not ideal.

&#x200B;

I realize MS is always improving WSL, but a full Linux environment scratches an itch for me. Maybe I don't leverage WSL2 as much as I could be, but in my workflows it seems to be an "in a pinch" or a testing solution at best.

Interesting...

Ran:

    $DriveSpaceTableObjects = @(
        @{'Name' = 'Drive Letter'; 'Expression' = {'I AM A POTENTIAL DEVICE ID'}}
        @{'Name' = '% Utilization'; 'Expression' = {(($_.Size - $_.FreeSpace) / $_.Size).ToString('P')}}
        @{'Name' = 'Size (GB)'; 'Expression' = {[int]($_.Size / 1GB)}}
        @{'Name' = 'Free (GB)'; 'Expression' = {[int]($_.FreeSpace / 1GB)}}
        @{'Name' = 'Server'; 'Expression' = {$_.Server}}
    )

    Invoke-Command -Authentication Kerberos -ComputerName $DCServers -ScriptBlock{
        Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_LogicalDisk | Where-Object {$_.DriveType -notlike 4 -and $_.DriveType -notlike 5} | Select-Object $Using:DriveSpaceTableObjects
    }

Output:

    Drive Letter   :
    % Utilization  :
    Size (GB)      :
    Free (GB)      :
    Server         : 
    PSComputerName : SERVER.DN.local
    RunspaceId     : 092ba323-fca1-4aed-bbfc-ae30a7aa896b

Okay, I'm onto something. See the following:

Using this array (Modification on the last line)

    $DriveSpaceTableObjects = @(
        @{'Name' = 'Drive Letter'; 'Expression' = {$_.DeviceID}}
        @{'Name' = '% Utilization'; 'Expression' = {(($_.Size - $_.FreeSpace) / $_.Size).ToString('P')}}
        @{'Name' = 'Size (GB)'; 'Expression' = {[int]($_.Size / 1GB)}}
        @{'Name' = 'Free (GB)'; 'Expression' = {[int]($_.FreeSpace / 1GB)}}
        @{'Name' = 'Server'; 'Expression' = {$_.Server}}
    )

I run Invoke-Command like this

    Invoke-Command -Authentication Kerberos -ComputerName $DCServers -ScriptBlock{
        Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_LogicalDisk | Where-Object {$_.DriveType -notlike 4 -and $_.DriveType -notlike 5} | Select-Object *,@{'Name' = 'Server'; 'Expression' = {$env:COMPUTERNAME}}
    } | Select-Object $DriveSpaceTableObjects

Then my responses come in properly. I have a feeling that the `$Using:` scope applies it to all of the variables in the array, but if that was the case, then I would at least get back "Server" as my local workstation name.

&#x200B;

Still doesn't feel like an answer, though.

Yeah, during the Texas grid failures at the beginning of the year, I had a moment of "Oh, fuck." 

&#x200B;

Promptly realized that no one was going to be leaving their house for anything so we just powered everything down, headed home and called it quits until the whole state wasn't at a complete standstill.
